prompt:
In the life_review/ folder on the desktop is my life data from the past year: calendar.ics (schedule), notes.md (casual notes), spending.csv (spending records), travel_records.csv (travel records), journal/ (personal diary, by month), and todo_list.md.
Help me review the important decisions I made over the past year — for example, changing gyms, buying equipment, taking courses, traveling, subscribing to services, adjusting work arrangements, etc.
For each decision:
1. Find out from the data when the decision was made and what the background/goal was at the time (I wrote my thoughts at the time in my diary and notes);
2. Find out what actually happened afterwards (how many times I actually went, how much I used it, how much time I spent);
3. Compare expected outcome vs actual outcome, and judge whether this decision met expectations;
4. If I could do it again, give advice based on the preferences I recorded.
Note: the data will not directly say “这是一个决定”, so you need to identify them yourself; also do not simply judge success or failure by how much money was spent — combine it with the goal at the time.
Finally, summarize my recurring decision patterns (for example, always overestimating my usage frequency), and output to /root/Desktop/life_review/年度决策复盘.md.

